title: Row Models Guide
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you take a look at the most basic example of TanStack Table, you'll see a code snippet like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) // Core features only\n\nfunction Component() {\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v9, row model factories live on the features object created by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":". The core row model is always included automatically. You only add the row models you need for filtering, sorting, pagination, etc. This keeps your bundle small, since you only import and use the code for the features you enable."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-are-row-models","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What are Row Models?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row models run under the hood of TanStack Table to transform your original data in useful ways that are needed for data grid features like filtering, sorting, grouping, expanding, and pagination. The rows that get generated and are rendered on screen won't necessarily be a 1:1 mapping of the original data that you passed to the table. They may be sorted, filtered, paginated, etc."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuring-row-models","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuring Row Models"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You should only add the row models that you need. Pass the row model factories as slots directly inside your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" call alongside your feature objects:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Slot Key"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Factory Function"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Purpose"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(automatic)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(none)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core row model (always included)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filteredRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFilteredRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filtering (column + global)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSortedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paginatedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createPaginatedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expandedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createExpandedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row expanding"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createGroupedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping; aggregation when enabled"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facetedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFacetedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceted filtering"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facetedMinMaxValues"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFacetedMinMaxValues()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Min/max for faceted filters"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facetedUniqueValues"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFacetedUniqueValues()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unique values for faceted filters"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The factory functions no longer accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":" as arguments. Those function maps are registered as their own named slots on the features object (see "},{"type":"link","href":"#function-registries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Function Registries"}]},{"type":"text","value":" below)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You must also add the corresponding feature objects to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" for each row model you use. For example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the full built-in registries ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":") can still be spread into these slots, but they are deprecated because they pull every built-in function into your bundle. Register only the functions you use under their conventional string keys, or pass functions directly to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" column options with no registration at all. String keys, including the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'auto'"},{"type":"text","value":", only resolve functions that are registered."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"function-registries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Function Registries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts three named registry slots: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":". Each slot is an object whose keys become valid, fully type-safe string values for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilterFn"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" in your column definitions and table options. You only pay for the functions you register."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst myFuzzyFilter: FilterFn<typeof features, Person> = (\n  row,\n  columnId,\n  value,\n  addMeta,\n) => {\n  // ...\n  return true\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { fuzzy: myFuzzyFilter },\n})\n\n// 'fuzzy' is now a valid type-safe value for filterFn in column defs:\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\ncolumnHelper.accessor('name', { filterFn: 'fuzzy' })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same pattern applies for sorting and grouping:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns: { myCustomSort }"},{"type":"text","value":" makes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'myCustomSort'"},{"type":"text","value":" valid for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" in column defs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns: { myAgg }"},{"type":"text","value":" makes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'myAgg'"},{"type":"text","value":" valid for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" in column defs."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To use built-in functions by string name, import them individually ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn_includesString"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn_alphanumeric"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn_sum"},{"type":"text","value":", and so on) and register them under their conventional keys, e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString }"},{"type":"text","value":". If a column option accepts a function directly, you can also skip registration entirely and pass the imported function as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" value."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"customize-fork-row-models","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Customize/Fork Row Models"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You don't have to use the exact row models that are provided by TanStack Table. If you need some advanced customization for certain row models, feel free to copy the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/tree/main/packages/table-core/src/features","children":[{"type":"text","value":"source code"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the row model you want to customize and modify it to your needs. Each row model factory lives alongside its feature (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row-sorting/createSortedRowModel.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"), and the core row model lives in "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/table/tree/main/packages/table-core/src/core/row-models","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"core/row-models"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-row-models","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using Row Models"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once your table instance has been created, you can access all of the row models that you may need directly from the table instance. There are even more derived row models available apart from the ones that you may have imported."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For normal rendering use cases, you will probably only need to use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getRowModel()"},{"type":"text","value":" method, as this row model will use all/any of the other row models depending on which features you have enabled or disabled. All of the other row models are available for you to \"dig into\" some of the underlying data transformations that are happening in the table."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"available-row-models-on-table-instance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available Row Models on Table Instance"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getRowModel"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - This is the main row model that you should use for rendering your table rows markup. It will use all of the other row models to generate the final row model that you will use to render your table rows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCoreRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a basic row model that is just a 1:1 mapping of the original data passed to the table."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFilteredRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model that accounts for column filtering and global filtering."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPreFilteredRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model before column filtering and global filtering are applied."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getGroupedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model that applies grouping and creates sub-rows. When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowAggregationFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" is also registered, configured aggregate values are computed for those grouped rows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPreGroupedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns the row model before grouping. Its root rows are the default row set used by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAggregationValue()"},{"type":"text","value":" for grand totals; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxAggregationDepth"},{"type":"text","value":" can select a deeper frontier."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getSortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model that has had sorting applied to it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPreSortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model before sorting is applied (rows are in original order)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getExpandedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model that accounts for expanded/hidden sub-rows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPreExpandedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model that only includes root level rows with no expanded sub-rows included. Still includes sorting."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPaginatedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model that only includes the rows that should be displayed on the current page based on the pagination state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPrePaginatedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model without pagination applied (includes all rows)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getSelectedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model of all selected rows (but only based on the data that was passed to the table). Runs after getCoreRowModel."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPreSelectedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model before row selection is applied (Just returns getCoreRowModel)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getGroupedSelectedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model of selected rows after grouping. Runs after getSortedRowModel, which runs after getGroupedRowModel, which runs after getFilteredRowModel."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFilteredSelectedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" - returns a row model of selected rows after column filtering and global filtering. Runs after getFilteredRowModel."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-order-of-row-model-execution","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Order of Row Model Execution"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Knowing how TanStack Table processes rows internally can help you gain a better understanding of what is happening under the hood, and help you debug any issues you may encounter."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Internally, this is the order in which each of the row models are applied to the data, if their respective features are enabled:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCoreRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" -> "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFilteredRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" -> "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getGroupedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" -> "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getSortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" -> "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getExpandedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" -> "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPaginatedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" -> "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getRowModel"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If in any case the respective feature is disabled or turned off with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"manual*\""},{"type":"text","value":" table option, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPre*RowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" will be used instead in that step of the process."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As you can see above, first the data is filtered, then grouped, then sorted, then expanded, and then finally paginated as the final step."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"row-model-data-structure","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Model Data Structure"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each row model will provide you the rows in 3 different useful formats:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rows"},{"type":"text","value":" - An array of rows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flatRows"},{"type":"text","value":" - An array of rows, but all sub-rows are flattened into the top level."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowsById"},{"type":"text","value":" - An object of rows, where each row is keyed by its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":". This is useful for quickly looking up rows by their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" with better performance."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"console.log(table.getRowModel().rows) // array of rows\nconsole.log(table.getRowModel().flatRows) // array of rows, but all sub-rows are flattened into the top level\nconsole.log(table.getRowModel().rowsById['row-id']) // object of rows, where each row is keyed by its `id`","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[{"id":"what-are-row-models","text":"What are Row Models?","level":2},{"id":"configuring-row-models","text":"Configuring Row Models","level":2},{"id":"function-registries","text":"Function Registries","level":2},{"id":"customize-fork-row-models","text":"Customize/Fork Row Models","level":2},{"id":"using-row-models","text":"Using Row Models","level":2},{"id":"available-row-models-on-table-instance","text":"Available Row Models on Table Instance","level":2},{"id":"the-order-of-row-model-execution","text":"The Order of Row Model Execution","level":2},{"id":"row-model-data-structure","text":"Row Model Data Structure","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nIf you take a look at the most basic example of TanStack Table, you'll see a code snippet like this:\n\n```ts\nimport { tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) // Core features only\n\nfunction Component() {\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n}\n```\n\nIn v9, row model factories live on the features object created by `tableFeatures()`. The core row model is always included automatically. You only add the row models you need for filtering, sorting, pagination, etc. This keeps your bundle small, since you only import and use the code for the features you enable.\n\n## What are Row Models?\n\nRow models run under the hood of TanStack Table to transform your original data in useful ways that are needed for data grid features like filtering, sorting, grouping, expanding, and pagination. The rows that get generated and are rendered on screen won't necessarily be a 1:1 mapping of the original data that you passed to the table. They may be sorted, filtered, paginated, etc.\n\n## Configuring Row Models\n\nYou should only add the row models that you need. Pass the row model factories as slots directly inside your `tableFeatures()` call alongside your feature objects:\n\n| Slot Key              | Factory Function              | Purpose                            |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| (automatic)           | (none)                        | Core row model (always included)   |\n| `filteredRowModel`    | `createFilteredRowModel()`    | Filtering (column + global)        |\n| `sortedRowModel`      | `createSortedRowModel()`      | Sorting                            |\n| `paginatedRowModel`   | `createPaginatedRowModel()`   | Pagination                         |\n| `expandedRowModel`    | `createExpandedRowModel()`    | Row expanding                      |\n| `groupedRowModel`     | `createGroupedRowModel()`     | Grouping; aggregation when enabled |\n| `facetedRowModel`     | `createFacetedRowModel()`     | Faceted filtering                  |\n| `facetedMinMaxValues` | `createFacetedMinMaxValues()` | Min/max for faceted filters        |\n| `facetedUniqueValues` | `createFacetedUniqueValues()` | Unique values for faceted filters  |\n\nThe factory functions no longer accept `filterFns`, `sortFns`, or `aggregationFns` as arguments. Those function maps are registered as their own named slots on the features object (see [Function Registries](#function-registries) below).\n\nYou must also add the corresponding feature objects to `tableFeatures()` for each row model you use. For example:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> the full built-in registries (`filterFns`, `sortFns`, `aggregationFns`) can still be spread into these slots, but they are deprecated because they pull every built-in function into your bundle. Register only the functions you use under their conventional string keys, or pass functions directly to the `filterFn`, `sortFn`, and `aggregationFn` column options with no registration at all. String keys, including the default `'auto'`, only resolve functions that are registered.\n\n## Function Registries\n\n`tableFeatures()` accepts three named registry slots: `filterFns`, `sortFns`, and `aggregationFns`. Each slot is an object whose keys become valid, fully type-safe string values for `filterFn`, `sortFn`, `globalFilterFn`, and `aggregationFn` in your column definitions and table options. You only pay for the functions you register.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst myFuzzyFilter: FilterFn<typeof features, Person> = (\n  row,\n  columnId,\n  value,\n  addMeta,\n) => {\n  // ...\n  return true\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { fuzzy: myFuzzyFilter },\n})\n\n// 'fuzzy' is now a valid type-safe value for filterFn in column defs:\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\ncolumnHelper.accessor('name', { filterFn: 'fuzzy' })\n```\n\nThe same pattern applies for sorting and grouping:\n\n- `sortFns: { myCustomSort }` makes `'myCustomSort'` valid for `sortFn` in column defs.\n- `aggregationFns: { myAgg }` makes `'myAgg'` valid for `aggregationFn` in column defs.\n\nTo use built-in functions by string name, import them individually (`filterFn_includesString`, `sortFn_alphanumeric`, `aggregationFn_sum`, and so on) and register them under their conventional keys, e.g. `filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString }`. If a column option accepts a function directly, you can also skip registration entirely and pass the imported function as the `filterFn`, `sortFn`, or `aggregationFn` value.\n\n## Customize/Fork Row Models\n\nYou don't have to use the exact row models that are provided by TanStack Table. If you need some advanced customization for certain row models, feel free to copy the [source code](https://github.com/TanStack/table/tree/main/packages/table-core/src/features) for the row model you want to customize and modify it to your needs. Each row model factory lives alongside its feature (e.g. `row-sorting/createSortedRowModel.ts`), and the core row model lives in [`core/row-models`](https://github.com/TanStack/table/tree/main/packages/table-core/src/core/row-models).\n\n## Using Row Models\n\nOnce your table instance has been created, you can access all of the row models that you may need directly from the table instance. There are even more derived row models available apart from the ones that you may have imported.\n\nFor normal rendering use cases, you will probably only need to use the `table.getRowModel()` method, as this row model will use all/any of the other row models depending on which features you have enabled or disabled. All of the other row models are available for you to \"dig into\" some of the underlying data transformations that are happening in the table.\n\n## Available Row Models on Table Instance\n\n- **`getRowModel`** - This is the main row model that you should use for rendering your table rows markup. It will use all of the other row models to generate the final row model that you will use to render your table rows.\n\n- `getCoreRowModel` - returns a basic row model that is just a 1:1 mapping of the original data passed to the table.\n\n- `getFilteredRowModel` - returns a row model that accounts for column filtering and global filtering.\n- `getPreFilteredRowModel` - returns a row model before column filtering and global filtering are applied.\n\n- `getGroupedRowModel` - returns a row model that applies grouping and creates sub-rows. When `rowAggregationFeature` is also registered, configured aggregate values are computed for those grouped rows.\n- `getPreGroupedRowModel` - returns the row model before grouping. Its root rows are the default row set used by `column.getAggregationValue()` for grand totals; `maxAggregationDepth` can select a deeper frontier.\n\n- `getSortedRowModel` - returns a row model that has had sorting applied to it.\n- `getPreSortedRowModel` - returns a row model before sorting is applied (rows are in original order).\n\n- `getExpandedRowModel` - returns a row model that accounts for expanded/hidden sub-rows.\n- `getPreExpandedRowModel` - returns a row model that only includes root level rows with no expanded sub-rows included. Still includes sorting.\n\n- `getPaginatedRowModel` - returns a row model that only includes the rows that should be displayed on the current page based on the pagination state.\n- `getPrePaginatedRowModel` - returns a row model without pagination applied (includes all rows).\n\n- `getSelectedRowModel` - returns a row model of all selected rows (but only based on the data that was passed to the table). Runs after getCoreRowModel.\n- `getPreSelectedRowModel` - returns a row model before row selection is applied (Just returns getCoreRowModel).\n- `getGroupedSelectedRowModel` - returns a row model of selected rows after grouping. Runs after getSortedRowModel, which runs after getGroupedRowModel, which runs after getFilteredRowModel.\n- `getFilteredSelectedRowModel` - returns a row model of selected rows after column filtering and global filtering. Runs after getFilteredRowModel.\n\n## The Order of Row Model Execution\n\nKnowing how TanStack Table processes rows internally can help you gain a better understanding of what is happening under the hood, and help you debug any issues you may encounter.\n\nInternally, this is the order in which each of the row models are applied to the data, if their respective features are enabled:\n\n`getCoreRowModel` -> `getFilteredRowModel` -> `getGroupedRowModel` -> `getSortedRowModel` -> `getExpandedRowModel` -> `getPaginatedRowModel` -> `getRowModel`\n\nIf in any case the respective feature is disabled or turned off with a `\"manual*\"` table option, the `getPre*RowModel` will be used instead in that step of the process.\n\nAs you can see above, first the data is filtered, then grouped, then sorted, then expanded, and then finally paginated as the final step.\n\n## Row Model Data Structure\n\nEach row model will provide you the rows in 3 different useful formats:\n\n1. `rows` - An array of rows.\n2. `flatRows` - An array of rows, but all sub-rows are flattened into the top level.\n3. `rowsById` - An object of rows, where each row is keyed by its `id`. This is useful for quickly looking up rows by their `id` with better performance.\n\n```ts\nconsole.log(table.getRowModel().rows) // array of rows\nconsole.log(table.getRowModel().flatRows) // array of rows, but all sub-rows are flattened into the top level\nconsole.log(table.getRowModel().rowsById['row-id']) // object of rows, where each row is keyed by its `id`\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Row Models Guide\n---\n\nIf you take a look at the most basic example of TanStack Table, you'll see a code snippet like this:\n\n```ts\nimport { tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({}) // Core features only\n\nfunction Component() {\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n}\n```\n\nIn v9, row model factories live on the features object created by `tableFeatures()`. The core row model is always included automatically. You only add the row models you need for filtering, sorting, pagination, etc. This keeps your bundle small, since you only import and use the code for the features you enable.\n\n## What are Row Models?\n\nRow models run under the hood of TanStack Table to transform your original data in useful ways that are needed for data grid features like filtering, sorting, grouping, expanding, and pagination. The rows that get generated and are rendered on screen won't necessarily be a 1:1 mapping of the original data that you passed to the table. They may be sorted, filtered, paginated, etc.\n\n## Configuring Row Models\n\nYou should only add the row models that you need. Pass the row model factories as slots directly inside your `tableFeatures()` call alongside your feature objects:\n\n| Slot Key              | Factory Function              | Purpose                            |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| (automatic)           | (none)                        | Core row model (always included)   |\n| `filteredRowModel`    | `createFilteredRowModel()`    | Filtering (column + global)        |\n| `sortedRowModel`      | `createSortedRowModel()`      | Sorting                            |\n| `paginatedRowModel`   | `createPaginatedRowModel()`   | Pagination                         |\n| `expandedRowModel`    | `createExpandedRowModel()`    | Row expanding                      |\n| `groupedRowModel`     | `createGroupedRowModel()`     | Grouping; aggregation when enabled |\n| `facetedRowModel`     | `createFacetedRowModel()`     | Faceted filtering                  |\n| `facetedMinMaxValues` | `createFacetedMinMaxValues()` | Min/max for faceted filters        |\n| `facetedUniqueValues` | `createFacetedUniqueValues()` | Unique values for faceted filters  |\n\nThe factory functions no longer accept `filterFns`, `sortFns`, or `aggregationFns` as arguments. Those function maps are registered as their own named slots on the features object (see [Function Registries](#function-registries) below).\n\nYou must also add the corresponding feature objects to `tableFeatures()` for each row model you use. For example:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> the full built-in registries (`filterFns`, `sortFns`, `aggregationFns`) can still be spread into these slots, but they are deprecated because they pull every built-in function into your bundle. Register only the functions you use under their conventional string keys, or pass functions directly to the `filterFn`, `sortFn`, and `aggregationFn` column options with no registration at all. String keys, including the default `'auto'`, only resolve functions that are registered.\n\n## Function Registries\n\n`tableFeatures()` accepts three named registry slots: `filterFns`, `sortFns`, and `aggregationFns`. Each slot is an object whose keys become valid, fully type-safe string values for `filterFn`, `sortFn`, `globalFilterFn`, and `aggregationFn` in your column definitions and table options. You only pay for the functions you register.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst myFuzzyFilter: FilterFn<typeof features, Person> = (\n  row,\n  columnId,\n  value,\n  addMeta,\n) => {\n  // ...\n  return true\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { fuzzy: myFuzzyFilter },\n})\n\n// 'fuzzy' is now a valid type-safe value for filterFn in column defs:\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\ncolumnHelper.accessor('name', { filterFn: 'fuzzy' })\n```\n\nThe same pattern applies for sorting and grouping:\n\n- `sortFns: { myCustomSort }` makes `'myCustomSort'` valid for `sortFn` in column defs.\n- `aggregationFns: { myAgg }` makes `'myAgg'` valid for `aggregationFn` in column defs.\n\nTo use built-in functions by string name, import them individually (`filterFn_includesString`, `sortFn_alphanumeric`, `aggregationFn_sum`, and so on) and register them under their conventional keys, e.g. `filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString }`. If a column option accepts a function directly, you can also skip registration entirely and pass the imported function as the `filterFn`, `sortFn`, or `aggregationFn` value.\n\n## Customize/Fork Row Models\n\nYou don't have to use the exact row models that are provided by TanStack Table. If you need some advanced customization for certain row models, feel free to copy the [source code](https://github.com/TanStack/table/tree/main/packages/table-core/src/features) for the row model you want to customize and modify it to your needs. Each row model factory lives alongside its feature (e.g. `row-sorting/createSortedRowModel.ts`), and the core row model lives in [`core/row-models`](https://github.com/TanStack/table/tree/main/packages/table-core/src/core/row-models).\n\n## Using Row Models\n\nOnce your table instance has been created, you can access all of the row models that you may need directly from the table instance. There are even more derived row models available apart from the ones that you may have imported.\n\nFor normal rendering use cases, you will probably only need to use the `table.getRowModel()` method, as this row model will use all/any of the other row models depending on which features you have enabled or disabled. All of the other row models are available for you to \"dig into\" some of the underlying data transformations that are happening in the table.\n\n## Available Row Models on Table Instance\n\n- **`getRowModel`** - This is the main row model that you should use for rendering your table rows markup. It will use all of the other row models to generate the final row model that you will use to render your table rows.\n\n- `getCoreRowModel` - returns a basic row model that is just a 1:1 mapping of the original data passed to the table.\n\n- `getFilteredRowModel` - returns a row model that accounts for column filtering and global filtering.\n- `getPreFilteredRowModel` - returns a row model before column filtering and global filtering are applied.\n\n- `getGroupedRowModel` - returns a row model that applies grouping and creates sub-rows. When `rowAggregationFeature` is also registered, configured aggregate values are computed for those grouped rows.\n- `getPreGroupedRowModel` - returns the row model before grouping. Its root rows are the default row set used by `column.getAggregationValue()` for grand totals; `maxAggregationDepth` can select a deeper frontier.\n\n- `getSortedRowModel` - returns a row model that has had sorting applied to it.\n- `getPreSortedRowModel` - returns a row model before sorting is applied (rows are in original order).\n\n- `getExpandedRowModel` - returns a row model that accounts for expanded/hidden sub-rows.\n- `getPreExpandedRowModel` - returns a row model that only includes root level rows with no expanded sub-rows included. Still includes sorting.\n\n- `getPaginatedRowModel` - returns a row model that only includes the rows that should be displayed on the current page based on the pagination state.\n- `getPrePaginatedRowModel` - returns a row model without pagination applied (includes all rows).\n\n- `getSelectedRowModel` - returns a row model of all selected rows (but only based on the data that was passed to the table). Runs after getCoreRowModel.\n- `getPreSelectedRowModel` - returns a row model before row selection is applied (Just returns getCoreRowModel).\n- `getGroupedSelectedRowModel` - returns a row model of selected rows after grouping. Runs after getSortedRowModel, which runs after getGroupedRowModel, which runs after getFilteredRowModel.\n- `getFilteredSelectedRowModel` - returns a row model of selected rows after column filtering and global filtering. Runs after getFilteredRowModel.\n\n## The Order of Row Model Execution\n\nKnowing how TanStack Table processes rows internally can help you gain a better understanding of what is happening under the hood, and help you debug any issues you may encounter.\n\nInternally, this is the order in which each of the row models are applied to the data, if their respective features are enabled:\n\n`getCoreRowModel` -> `getFilteredRowModel` -> `getGroupedRowModel` -> `getSortedRowModel` -> `getExpandedRowModel` -> `getPaginatedRowModel` -> `getRowModel`\n\nIf in any case the respective feature is disabled or turned off with a `\"manual*\"` table option, the `getPre*RowModel` will be used instead in that step of the process.\n\nAs you can see above, first the data is filtered, then grouped, then sorted, then expanded, and then finally paginated as the final step.\n\n## Row Model Data Structure\n\nEach row model will provide you the rows in 3 different useful formats:\n\n1. `rows` - An array of rows.\n2. `flatRows` - An array of rows, but all sub-rows are flattened into the top level.\n3. `rowsById` - An object of rows, where each row is keyed by its `id`. This is useful for quickly looking up rows by their `id` with better performance.\n\n```ts\nconsole.log(table.getRowModel().rows) // array of rows\nconsole.log(table.getRowModel().flatRows) // array of rows, but all sub-rows are flattened into the top level\nconsole.log(table.getRowModel().rowsById['row-id']) // object of rows, where each row is keyed by its `id`\n```\n"}
